[size=16pt][b]Bitcoin Machine Code vs Bitcoin Core, module by module: 31 of Core's own benchmarks, side by side[/b][/size]

2026-09-27. Bitcoin Machine Code (an all-assembly x86-64 node, [url]https://github.com/BobClawblaw/bitcoinmachinecode[/url]) measured against Bitcoin Core's own benchmark suite ([tt]src/bench[/tt], v31.99.0-67efced1fc83), on the same box, the same core, the same inputs.

[b]How it was measured.[/b] Core's numbers come from Core's [tt]bench_bitcoin[/tt] (nanobench, [tt]-min-time=1000[/tt], the minimum over 3 processes), built RelWithDebInfo as Core's own default; the secp256k1 rows from libsecp256k1's own [tt]bench[/tt], Core's vendored copy with Core's shipped config. Our numbers come from harnesses that mirror the shape of each Core benchmark -- the same work per iteration, the same buffer sizes, the same element counts -- as thread CPU time, the minimum of 15 rounds. Both sides pinned to one core ([tt]taskset -c 25[/tt]) of an AMD Ryzen 9 9950X3D. The production node and a Core oracle were running on the box at the time (load average 3.95 on 32 threads); Core's timed loops showed no preemption (cpu/wall 1.000 in every group). Where the two sides do not do the same work, the row says so.

