[tr][td]SHA-256, 1 MB[/td][td]0.359 ns/byte[/td][td]0.410 ns/byte[/td][td][b]1.14x slower[/b][/td][td]Core: SHA-NI[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]script verification, P2WPKH / P2TR key path[/td][td]20.06 / 20.28 us[/td][td]22.52 / 22.26 us[/td][td][b]1.12x / 1.10x slower[/b][/td][td]the signature verifications are level (above); Core precomputes the sighash midstates once per transaction, we hash per input[/td][/tr]
[/table]

[size=12pt][b]What is not in these tables, and why[/b][/size]

Core's [tt]src/bench[/tt] has 59 files. 31 benchmark names across 14 of them are paired above. The rest, with the reason:

[list]
[*][b]No counterpart by design:[/b] [tt]addrman[/tt] (a different address-book structure), [tt]ccoins_caching[/tt] (Core's coins cache is a different object from our memtable/LSM), [tt]checkqueue[/tt] (our parallel verify is measured whole-block), [tt]connectblock[/tt] (Core connects a synthetic block through its chainstate; ours is measured end to end by the IBD comparisons), [tt]rollingbloom[/tt], [tt]txgraph[/tt], [tt]txorphanage[/tt], [tt]disconnected_transactions[/tt], [tt]checkblockindex[/tt], [tt]load_external[/tt], [tt]index_blockfilter[/tt].
[*][b]Measured at the RPC level instead[/b] (the project's PERFORMANCE.md): [tt]block_assemble[/tt] ([tt]getblocktemplate[/tt]), [tt]rpc_blockchain[/tt], [tt]rpc_mempool[/tt].
[*][b]C++ runtime and utility micro-benchmarks with no equivalent module:[/b] [tt]prevector[/tt], [tt]pool[/tt], [tt]lockedpool[/tt], [tt]random[/tt], [tt]logging[/tt], [tt]streams_findbyte[/tt], [tt]strencodings[/tt], [tt]parse_hex[/tt], [tt]util_time[/tt], [tt]hashpadding[/tt], [tt]obfuscation[/tt], the harness itself.
[*][b]Different wallet:[/b] the seven [tt]wallet_*[/tt] benches (Core's descriptor wallet vs a journal-backed seed wallet).
[*][b]Candidates a day's harness each would pair:[/b] [tt]cluster_linearize[/tt], [tt]coin_selection[/tt], [tt]descriptors[/tt], the three [tt]mempool_*[/tt] eviction/stress benches, [tt]peer_eviction[/tt], [tt]sign_transaction[/tt].
[*][b]Partly paired:[/b] [tt]blockencodings[/tt] (we keep no extra-transaction pool, so only the NoExtra variant), [tt]gcs_filter[/tt] (construct and hash; we neither decode nor match filters), [tt]base58[/tt] (Base58Check encode only), [tt]readwriteblock[/tt] (raw read and write; Core's ReadBlock deserialises and checks too), [tt]verify_script[/tt] (3 of 4; VerifyNestedIfScript is interpreter-only).
[/list]

[size=12pt][b]What this says[/b][/size]

The consensus kernel -- signature verification, the hashes on the hot path, block reconstruction from a compact block, the block archive, the UTXO set's MuHash -- is level with Core or ahead. The gaps are in the parts still written in C on this side (ElligatorSwift, ChaCha20, the AEAD), in code that runs once per RPC call (the MuHash finalize inverse), in the two hashes nothing hot uses (SHA-1, SHA-512), and in per-input sighash recomputation that Core amortises per transaction. Each gap is named with its cause so the next piece of assembly can be chosen by the number, not by guesswork.

[size=12pt][b]Reproducing[/b][/size]

[code]
git clone https://github.com/BobClawblaw/bitcoinmachinecode
cd bitcoinmachinecode && scripts/bench_vs_core.sh          # tiers 1-2, including 2b
[/code]

The script builds Core's [tt]bench_bitcoin[/tt] out of tree on first use, builds our harnesses ([tt]make bench-vs-core[/tt]), runs both sides pinned, and writes the raw CSVs, our harness output and the joined table into [tt]bench-results/<timestamp>/[/tt]. The harness is [tt]asm/tests/bench_core_modules.c[/tt]; every difference between a pair is stated in its file header and in the table's note column. The full write-up, including the classification above and the earlier tiers, is [tt]docs/devlog/BENCHMARKS.md[/tt], addendum 2026-09-27.
